# Build context is the repository root (docker build -f docker/Dockerfile .).
#
# Everything excluded here either bloats the context or would copy host build output into the image,
# where it can shadow what the SDK stage produces — a stale bin/ is a genuinely confusing way to
# discover that your fix did not take.

**/bin/
**/obj/
build/
publish/
artifacts/
TestResults/

# Runtime data. The wildcard matters for the same reason it does in .gitignore: a sibling backup such
# as App_Data.bak-2026-01-01/ holds the Data Protection master key in UNENCRYPTED form, and that key
# decrypts every stored secret and signs auth cookies. Copying one into an image that gets pushed to a
# public registry would disclose all of it.
**/App_Data*/
*.db
*.db-wal
*.db-shm
*.design.db*

# Editor, VCS and CI
.git/
.gitea/
.github/
.vs/
.idea/
.vscode/
*.user
*.suo

# Not needed to build or run the application. The Dockerfile is read from the host rather than the
# context, so excluding docker/ here does not stop it being used with -f.
docker/
_docs/
deploy/
scripts/
*.md

# The end-to-end battery. The Dockerfile copies only Core.MT-Uptime/ and SelfHost.MT-Uptime/ by name,
# so neither of these would reach the image anyway — they are listed for the same reason the entries
# above are: to keep the build context small and to say out loud that they are not part of the app.
# e2e/ additionally holds shell and Python that only ever runs on a prepared test box.
e2e/
Tests.E2E.MT-Uptime/
